---
/**
 * The app strip: one line, one compact Download, one dismiss. It rides at the
 * top of the sticky stack in `BaseLayout`, so it stays put as the page scrolls.
 *
 * ── IT MUST NOT OUTRANK "BOOK A BAY" ───────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The site's primary CTA is transactional and lives in the header directly
 * below this strip (playbook core.md §6: two clicks to book from any page). An
 * ember fill here would put a second ember button one row above the first and
 * make the visitor choose between downloading an app and booking a bay — which
 * is not a choice we want to offer, because only one of them is revenue today.
 *
 * So the Download control takes the `outline` variant: navy on greige, legible
 * at 13.31:1, unmistakably secondary to the ember button beneath it. Same
 * reasoning as the note on `SubscribeBand` in CLAUDE.md — a promo surface that
 * appears on every page earns its place by staying quiet.
 *
 * ── GROUND, AND WHY THERE IS NO MARK HERE ──────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Greige: paper is too close to the header's white to read as a separate
 * strip, so the stack is greige / white / midnight — three distinguishable
 * bands rather than a white sandwich.
 *
 * The strip carries NO logo. It shipped with one, and the only mark available
 * is the site wordmark the header renders one row below it — the same artwork
 * twice, a hand's width apart, which reads as a rendering fault rather than as
 * branding. The owner chose to drop it here rather than repeat it (2026-09-08).
 *
 * If a real app icon lands later, this is where it goes: square, `shrink-0`,
 * roughly `h-7`, first child of the row. A distinct icon earns its place
 * because it says "this is an app"; the wordmark said only "this is the site
 * you are already on". The footer badge keeps the wordmark, where nothing
 * competes with it.
 *
 * ── ONE BUTTON, TWO STORES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The client asked for a single compact Download. It is server-rendered to
 * Apple and rewritten to Google Play for an Android user agent by the inline
 * script below — so it is correct without JS for everyone except Android, and
 * correct with JS for everyone. The footer badge names both stores outright for
 * anyone the sniff gets wrong; that is the fallback, not this.
 *
 * ── DISMISSAL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The × sets a flag in `localStorage` and the strip stays gone on later pages
 * and later visits. The script is `is:inline` and sits immediately after the
 * markup on purpose: a bundled module script is deferred until after parse, and
 * a dismissed banner would flash into view on every page load before vanishing.
 * No cookie, nothing sent anywhere, and a visitor with storage disabled simply
 * keeps seeing the strip — which is the safe way for it to fail.
 */
import { buttonClasses } from '../../lib/buttons';
import { APP_STORE_URL, PLAY_STORE_URL, APP_PITCH, APP_NAME } from '../../lib/app';
---

<aside class="border-b-hairline border-ink/15 bg-greige" aria-label={`${APP_NAME} app`} data-app-banner>
  {
    /*
     * THIS ROW MUST NOT WRAP. Built with `flex-wrap` it measured 281px tall at
     * 375px — every child took its own line — and the sticky stack then stood
     * at 547px, two thirds of a phone screen, permanently. A promo strip that
     * costs the visitor most of their viewport on every page is worse than no
     * strip at all.
     *
     * So: one row, the sentence is the only thing allowed to take space
     * (`min-w-0 flex-1`), and everything else is `shrink-0`. The floor is the
     * 44px tap target inside the button, which is where it should be.
     */
  }
  <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-content items-center gap-x-s4 px-gutter py-s2 lg:gap-x-s5 lg:px-gutter-lg">
    <p class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-label-sm leading-body text-inkBody sm:text-small">{APP_PITCH}</p>

    <a
      href={APP_STORE_URL}
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      class={`${buttonClasses('sm', 'outline')} shrink-0`}
      data-app-store-link
      data-play-url={PLAY_STORE_URL}
      data-analytics="app-banner-download"
    >
      Download
    </a>

    <button
      type="button"
      class="flex min-h-tap min-w-tap shrink-0 items-center justify-center text-body text-inkMuted transition-colors duration-fast ease-standard hover:text-ink"
      data-app-dismiss
      aria-label={`Dismiss the ${APP_NAME} app message`}
    >
      <span aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
    </button>
  </div>
</aside>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var KEY = 'links:app-banner-dismissed';
    var banner = document.querySelector('[data-app-banner]');
    if (!banner) return;

    // Hide before paint if this visitor has already dismissed it.
    try {
      if (window.localStorage.getItem(KEY) === '1') banner.hidden = true;
    } catch {
      /* storage blocked — keep the strip, it is not worth an error */
    }

    // Android users get Google Play; everyone else keeps the rendered Apple link.
    try {
      if (/android/i.test(navigator.userAgent)) {
        var link = banner.querySelector('[data-app-store-link]');
        if (link && link.dataset.playUrl) link.href = link.dataset.playUrl;
      }
    } catch {
      /* keep the Apple link */
    }

    var dismiss = banner.querySelector('[data-app-dismiss]');
    if (dismiss) {
      dismiss.addEventListener('click', function () {
        banner.hidden = true;
        try {
          window.localStorage.setItem(KEY, '1');
        } catch {
          /* dismissed for this page view only */
        }
      });
    }
  })();
</script>
